// EXPECT: FAIL
// ERROR_CONTAINS: ANUBIS_
// A callee's `ensures` is not propagated to the caller's `requires` discharge, so two contracts that
// are PROVABLY CONTRADICTORY are accepted:
//
//     neg() ensures(result < 0)      need(y) requires(y > 0)      need(neg())
//
// Both facts are declared and present in the same program. This is not "the solver could not prove
// it" — it is the solver holding a proof of violation and never composing the two halves.
//
// Discriminator — the literal form IS caught, so precondition checking works and only COMPOSITION is
// missing:
//     need(-5)          REJECT
//     need(neg())       ACCEPT, run prints -5     <- this fixture
//     let v = neg(); need(v)   ACCEPT, run prints -5
//
// NOT a carrier defect: the DIRECT call leaks identically, so the fn-value carriers are incidental
// here. `LANGUAGE.md:239-244` states that contracts compose and the caller must satisfy the callee's
// obligations; the ensures->requires direction is unimplemented.
fn neg() -> i64 ensures(result < 0) { return -5; }
fn need(y: i64) -> i64 requires(y > 0) { return y; }
fn main() { print(need(neg())); }
